decisions:
  - id: TD-2026-05-27-audio-visual
    decision: Audio becomes a first-class AudioVisual layer; State.audio removed entirely
    rationale: v1 multi-track wanted audio to participate in tracks, parenting, and the timeline like video clips. Per-state audio couldn't express layered audio (background music + voiceover + SFX) and conflicted with the layer-uniform mental model. Pre-v1 docs that carry state.audio silently strip on read via Zod default.
  - id: TD-2026-05-27-project-kind
    decision: 'Top-level kind: video|image|carousel field (default video for backcompat)'
    rationale: Three creator-facing project flavors are distinct enough that exporters and authoring tools branch on kind. Default video keeps every pre-v1 doc working without migration.
  - id: TD-2026-05-27-carousel-slides-as-refs
    decision: Carousel slides are SlideRef[] pointing at any .atelier in workspace; no ownership concept; resolver enforces cycle safety
    rationale: Reuses the existing RefVisual composability hint. Slides are independently-editable .atelier files. Owner explicitly rejected slide-folder ownership — carousel is a manifest, not a container.
  - id: TD-2026-05-27-design-no-cascade
    decision: Per-file design block is self-contained — no auto-merge with workspace DESIGN. Lineage is explicit via relatedTo[]
    rationale: 'Owner rejected override/cascade semantics: ''I don''t believe in overriding any of these files but I want agents to see which design files relate to which and where they stem from.'' Resolution-at-distance is replaced by an explicit graph of pointers agents can walk.'
  - id: TD-2026-05-27-clip-local-captions
    decision: Captions parent to their clip; renderer shifts delta ranges by the nearest ancestor VideoVisual's startFrame
    rationale: The durable answer (vs. baking absolute frame offsets at write time). Move-a-clip-and-captions-follow works naturally. Renderer change is one helper + one frame-offset application; tests proved it preserves all existing single-state behavior (clip-local offset = 0 for layers without video ancestors).
  - id: TD-2026-05-27-tracks-as-group-convention
    decision: Video tracks are Group layers tagged ['track','video'] with parentId-parented clips; lazy-created on first clip ingest
    rationale: The schema already supports parentId + Group; no new top-level Track concept needed. Audio tracks would have needed real schema work (AudioVisual added separately addresses the audio-track motivation, layer-scoped). Lazy creation keeps fresh projects clean.
  - id: TD-2026-05-27-timeline-block-derived
    decision: Timeline summary block is writer-emitted, reader-tolerant. Layers stay source of truth
    rationale: 'Solves ''where are my clips in this 200-line .atelier file?'' without inventing a second canonical structure. Drift-safe: stale block is harmless because the layers are still authoritative.'
learnings:
  - Multi-track video is essentially free via Group + parentId — the schema was already composable. Multi-track AUDIO required real schema work (no AudioVisual existed). Owner's instinct toward 'multi-track' had two very different bills underneath; surfacing that distinction unlocked the right scope decision.
  - Adding new schema bundles late in a session works when the decisions are tight and the work is mechanical (Zod mirrors). Carousel/image/design landed in the same commit as multi-track because the type foundation was already in motion.
  - 'The ''design lineage not cascade'' framing is a strong concept: explicit relatedTo[] pointers replace spooky-resolution-at-distance. Worth applying to other ''inheritance'' surfaces in the framework.'
  - Builder subagents are well-suited for tightly-scoped rewrites with locked design + clear test expectations. The compose-video-project rewrite (608 lines) delegated cleanly and came back passing.
  - Pre-existing tsc errors blocking 'clean' commit often turn out to be stale dist .d.ts files — rebuilding the upstream package resolves them. Worth checking before assuming a real bug.
